#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "113fa98b-7e90-5296-b84b-7ad73c92c7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375400} {:answer/id #uuid "4c84bfa9-2b9f-5e84-a7f3-342b0bb1c5a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375401} {:answer/id #uuid "d7893014-dd5c-5f6e-b93d-500e216b395e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375402} {:answer/id #uuid "e03b4be3-5c12-534c-a489-a813734e31e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375403} {:answer/id #uuid "f47e8030-9b2f-556b-bc14-9ddcf243d206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c84bfa9-2b9f-5e84-a7f3-342b0bb1c5a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375401} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c314077-8489-5814-b6ff-baba0d5a0057" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed79e8c4-1810-5f03-9522-5e9ee679d4fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-162397" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3},\\,1-t⟩$ at $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{3},\\,y(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =3{t}^{2},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =6t,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|3{t}^{2}\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 6t|}{{((3t)^{2}+(1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6|t|}{{(9{t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{6|1|}{{(9(1)^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{(10)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{1}{κ(1)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e31722f-307c-5cf7-92ad-6f573d132cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375407} {:answer/id #uuid "773def70-5a39-5307-9598-15eab7739e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375408} {:answer/id #uuid "508ecb4c-d393-56c5-a1af-61933c093906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375409} {:answer/id #uuid "aae1ac6c-09e7-52fc-99b9-ee15048fd8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375410} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2ce6c9-53bf-5201-91e4-f367f6b28307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e31722f-307c-5cf7-92ad-6f573d132cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375407} :answer-field/id #uuid "93907cfe-e575-578b-afea-70371bb607a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29db884d-f24f-5424-973a-7e7c3098abfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-162404" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+(1-t)\\,j$ at $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-1,\\,y(t)=1-t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2t,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2t\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2|}{{((2t)^{2}+(-1)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(4{t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{{(4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f084a609-33b1-550f-8035-8938e3a8869f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375414} {:answer/id #uuid "833ae50e-3658-5883-b5aa-3d37893739de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375415} {:answer/id #uuid "d8eddfa4-0f66-5842-84a3-70b6639e2bd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375416} {:answer/id #uuid "ed71f44e-3adb-5719-a944-b27d60c25a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt[3]{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375417} {:answer/id #uuid "0c8e0038-3ef5-5231-8611-ed7c6a67146d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c8e0038-3ef5-5231-8611-ed7c6a67146d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375418} :answer-field/id #uuid "2696a603-3060-5aba-87e7-751979e9a935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59fdd5af-44ff-5ffd-94d6-223f05abf6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162405" :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=(1+t)^{2}\\,i+(1-t)^{2}\\,j$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1+t)^{2},\\,y(t)=(1-t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2(1+t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-2(1-t) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2(1+t)\\cdot 2-(-2(1-t))\\cdot 2|}{{(\\,(2(1+t))^{2}+(2(1-t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{(8+8{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{8}{{(8+8(0)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{(8+0)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c02e1f2a-12ee-5e0f-adc8-3dcc113e63ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375421} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3b0f1d-02ab-5f7f-b939-56146e3d4818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375422} {:answer/id #uuid "c254025a-acff-5311-a369-5cf0e944fc97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375423} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57e9dd-ba68-54ed-be3b-e96492c65ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375424} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad617fe-efc9-5076-8c27-09cc8636d85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c02e1f2a-12ee-5e0f-adc8-3dcc113e63ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375421} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3961c7-a34f-5cd8-b14a-5d66c9a394e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "158b5a12-e0e4-56c7-80c7-4700e06df09f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162532" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ at $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{[1+{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{{[1+{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{2}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384072a8-f008-5279-bb58-8086eb42b710" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ae907fd-e176-5a15-9783-082a6686e768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-e+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375428} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6ee906-ce06-59af-9494-631588201eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375429} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7924ef-f982-5292-8cbc-dee50f7629eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-e+2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375430} {:answer/id #uuid "0b95d3db-c026-5d02-903f-53dbdeb7c332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-e+2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375431} {:answer/id #uuid "517b8ae7-f429-5017-af57-43ba0b92d5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac6ee906-ce06-59af-9494-631588201eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375429} :answer-field/id #uuid "9479c27e-6a60-5b76-9bb7-d18536fff57c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9efc8285-3803-5a6e-9693-e78f932370e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162535" :question/problem "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=x-{e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=1-{e}^{x},\\,{y}^{″}(x)=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{-{e}^{x}}{{[1+{(1-{e}^{x})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{(1+1+{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{x})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{(2+{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{x})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(1) & =-\\frac{{e}^{(1)}}{{(2+{e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{(1)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
